module

public import StacksFrontier.Deformation.Examples.Linear.NodalCubicQColumn

/-!
# The counterexample diagram of Stacks 0DZ2: the lower row and the structural maps

Fourth layer of the instantiation of `Deformation.FlatModuleLiftBaseChangeStatement`. Layers one
to three built

`A' = NC[t]/t³`,  `A = NC`,  `P' = A'[x]/(x² − t·x − t²·Y)`,  `P = P'/t·P'`,
`Q₁ = P'[z]/(z² + z − Y)`,  `Q' = (Q₁)_{z+1}`,

and the lift `N'` on the right. This layer adds `Q = Q'/t·Q'`, the map `P → Q` between the two
reductions, the two scalar towers through it, and `Module.Flat A' Q'`.

## What the instance graph gives for free

The probe worth recording, because 0DZ2's Noetherian hypotheses are the ones a reader expects to
cost something: **`IsNoetherianRing` resolves automatically for all five of `A'`, `P'`, `P`, `Q₁`
and `Q'`**, and so do `Algebra A' Q₁`, `Algebra A' Q'`, `Algebra NC P'`, `Algebra NC P`,
`Algebra A' P`, `Algebra P' P`, `Algebra Q₁ Q'`, and the towers
`IsScalarTower A' P' Q₁`, `IsScalarTower A' P' Q'`, `IsScalarTower A' P' P`,
`IsScalarTower NC P' P`. They come free from `AdjoinRoot`, `Ideal.Quotient` and `Localization`
composing, with nothing to declare and no diamond to manage.

What looked unfree is anything involving `P → Q`, since `P` and `Q` are quotients of *different*
rings. It turns out to be free too, **provided `Q` is spelled as the quotient by the mapped ideal
`(t·P').map (P' → Q')` rather than by `(t·Q')`** — the same ideal, by `map_span_tauP`, but only
the first spelling lets Mathlib's `Ideal.Quotient.algebraQuotientMapQuotient` be the `Algebra P Q`
instance and `Algebra.TensorProduct.quotIdealMapEquivQuotTensor` identify `Q` with `P ⊗_{P'} Q'`.
Building `P → Q` by hand instead and declaring `Algebra P Q` from it creates a competing instance
and a diamond, which is how this was found.

`Module.Flat A' Q'` is then `Module.Flat.trans` of `flat_PBase` and `flat_QBase_PBase`.

## Main definitions

* `Deformation.Examples.ArtinSchreier.QRed`: `Q = Q'/t·Q'`, the counterexample's lower-right ring.
* `Deformation.Examples.ArtinSchreier.qRedEquivTensor`: the identification `Q = P ⊗_{P'} Q'`.

## Main results

* `Deformation.Examples.ArtinSchreier.map_tauP`: the image of `t` from `P'` in `Q'` is the `t` of
  `Q'`, which is the side condition making `P → Q` well defined.
* `Deformation.Examples.ArtinSchreier.flat_QBase_ABase`: **`Module.Flat A' Q'`**, a hypothesis of
  Stacks 0DZ2.
* `Deformation.Examples.ArtinSchreier.qRedEquivTensor`, `flat_QRed_PRed`: **`Q = P ⊗_{P'} Q'`**
  and hence **`Module.Flat P Q`**, the fourth and last flatness hypothesis.
* `Deformation.Examples.ArtinSchreier.surjective_QBase_QRed`,
  `Deformation.Examples.ArtinSchreier.ker_QBase_QRed_eq_map`: `Q' → Q` is surjective with kernel
  the ideal generated by the image of `ker(A' → A)` — `hsurjQ` and `hkerQ`.

## References

* [Stacks Project, Tag 0DZ2](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0DZ2)
-/

@[expose] public noncomputable section

open Polynomial
open Deformation.Examples.MaurerCartanWindow
open scoped TensorProduct

universe u

namespace Deformation.Examples.ArtinSchreier

/-! ### `Q = Q'/t·Q'` -/

/-- The image of `t` in `Q'`, through the automatic `Algebra A' Q'`. -/
def tauQBase : QBase := algebraMap ABase QBase (tau NC)

/-- `Q = Q'/t·Q'`, the counterexample's lower-right ring.

It is written as the quotient by the **mapped** ideal `(t·P').map (P' → Q')` rather than by
`(t·Q')`, which `map_span_tauP` says is the same ideal. The two spellings are interchangeable
mathematically but not syntactically, and this one is what lets
`Algebra.TensorProduct.quotIdealMapEquivQuotTensor` apply on the nose, giving
`Q ≃ₐ[P] P ⊗_{P'} Q'` and hence `Module.Flat P Q` in two lines (`flat_QRed_PRed`). -/
abbrev QRed : Type := QBase ⧸ (Ideal.span {tauP}).map (algebraMap PBase QBase)

/-- **The image of `t·P'` in `Q'` is `t·Q'`.** This is the side condition that makes `P → Q`
well defined, and it is just the tower `IsScalarTower A' P' Q'`, which resolves automatically. -/
theorem map_tauP : algebraMap PBase QBase tauP = tauQBase := by
  show algebraMap PBase QBase (algebraMap ABase PBase (tau NC)) =
    algebraMap ABase QBase (tau NC)
  rw [← IsScalarTower.algebraMap_apply]

/-- The ideal `t·P'` maps to the ideal `t·Q'`. This is the bridge between the two spellings of
the ideal defining `Q`. -/
theorem map_span_tauP :
    (Ideal.span {tauP}).map (algebraMap PBase QBase) = Ideal.span {tauQBase} := by
  rw [Ideal.map_span]
  simp [map_tauP]

/-- `Q' → Q` is surjective. -/
theorem surjective_QBase_QRed : Function.Surjective (algebraMap QBase QRed) :=
  Ideal.Quotient.mk_surjective

/-- The kernel of `Q' → Q` is `(t·Q')`. -/
theorem ker_QBase_QRed : RingHom.ker (algebraMap QBase QRed) = Ideal.span {tauQBase} :=
  Ideal.mk_ker.trans map_span_tauP

/-- **`hkerQ` of Stacks 0DZ2**: the kernel of `Q' → Q` is the ideal generated by the image of the
kernel of `A' → A`. -/
theorem ker_QBase_QRed_eq_map :
    RingHom.ker (algebraMap QBase QRed) =
      (RingHom.ker redA).map (algebraMap ABase QBase) := by
  rw [ker_QBase_QRed, ker_redA, Ideal.map_span]
  simp [tauQBase]

/-! ### The map `P → Q` and its towers -/

/-- `t·Q'` dies in `Q`. -/
theorem algebraMap_tauQBase_eq_zero : algebraMap QBase QRed tauQBase = 0 :=
  Ideal.Quotient.eq_zero_iff_mem.2
    (map_span_tauP ▸ Ideal.mem_span_singleton_self tauQBase)

/-- **`IsScalarTower P' P Q`**, one of the seven towers of Stacks 0DZ2.

Writing `Q` as the quotient by the *mapped* ideal pays a second dividend here: Mathlib's
`Ideal.Quotient.algebraQuotientMapQuotient` is then the `Algebra P Q` instance, and the towers
come with it. Building `P → Q` by hand with `Ideal.Quotient.lift` and declaring
`Algebra P Q` from it — which is what the first version of this file did — produces a *competing*
instance and a diamond; the hand-built map is not needed at all. -/
theorem isScalarTower_PBase_PRed_QRed : IsScalarTower PBase PRed QRed := inferInstance

/-- **`IsScalarTower A P Q`**, another of the seven. -/
theorem isScalarTower_NC_PRed_QRed : IsScalarTower NC PRed QRed :=
  IsScalarTower.of_algebraMap_eq fun a => by
    rw [IsScalarTower.algebraMap_apply NC PBase PRed a,
      ← IsScalarTower.algebraMap_apply PBase PRed QRed,
      ← IsScalarTower.algebraMap_apply NC PBase QRed]

/-! ### `Module.Flat A' Q'` -/

/-- **`Module.Flat A' Q'`**, a hypothesis of Stacks 0DZ2: `P'` is flat over `A'` and `Q'` is flat
over `P'`, both already proved, and the tower `IsScalarTower A' P' Q'` is automatic. -/
theorem flat_QBase_ABase : Module.Flat ABase QBase := by
  have := flat_PBase
  have := flat_QBase_PBase
  exact Module.Flat.trans ABase PBase QBase

/-! ### `Module.Flat P Q`, through `Q = P ⊗_{P'} Q'` -/

/-- **`Q = P ⊗_{P'} Q'` as `P`-algebras.** This is Mathlib's quotient base change,
`Algebra.TensorProduct.quotIdealMapEquivQuotTensor`, applied on the nose — which is the whole
reason `Q` is spelled with the mapped ideal. The `P`-algebra structure on both sides is
Mathlib's, so the linearity is the one `Module.Flat.of_linearEquiv` needs; a `P'`-linear equiv
would not have been enough. -/
def qRedEquivTensor : QRed ≃ₐ[PRed] (PRed ⊗[PBase] QBase) :=
  Algebra.TensorProduct.quotIdealMapEquivQuotTensor QBase (Ideal.span {tauP})

/-- **`Module.Flat P Q`**, the last of the four flatness hypotheses of Stacks 0DZ2. `Q'` is flat
over `P'`, so `P ⊗_{P'} Q'` is flat over `P` by `Module.Flat.baseChange`, and `Q` is that tensor
product. -/
theorem flat_QRed_PRed : Module.Flat PRed QRed := by
  have := flat_QBase_PBase
  have : Module.Flat PRed (PRed ⊗[PBase] QBase) := Module.Flat.baseChange PBase PRed QBase
  exact Module.Flat.of_linearEquiv qRedEquivTensor.toLinearEquiv

end Deformation.Examples.ArtinSchreier

end
